WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
So far, our rules have used information already represented in fields. But consider

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:11.011
these two delayed records. One note says customs has cleared, yet nobody will arrange

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:16.058
collection. The other says a required lab result is pending and names someone

00:00:16.058 --> 00:00:21.498
following up. The shared status does not explain the difference. An AI can read the

00:00:21.498 --> 00:00:26.872
permitted evidence and reason about whether an unresolved ownership handoff is the

00:00:26.872 --> 00:00:27.725
real problem.

00:00:32.225 --> 00:00:37.958
To use that interpretation in a rule, give it a structured form. Entity identifies the

00:00:37.958 --> 00:00:43.625
record it concerns. Attribute names what is being described. Value holds the proposed

00:00:43.625 --> 00:00:49.225
answer. This is the entity, attribute, value pattern, often shortened to E A V. Here

00:00:49.225 --> 00:00:54.225
the attribute is intervention, and the text value is ownership handoff. The

00:00:54.225 --> 00:00:59.491
explanation and citations make the interpretation inspectable. It remains an AI

00:00:59.491 --> 00:01:01.825
hypothesis, not a guaranteed truth.

00:01:06.325 --> 00:01:11.414
Now an agent can ask: what does intervention mean, and which values can I query? That

00:01:11.414 --> 00:01:16.504
meaning must come from an explicit attribute definition, not a guess about a name. In

00:01:16.504 --> 00:01:21.474
this example it distinguishes a missing ownership handoff from regulatory review or

00:01:21.474 --> 00:01:26.504
insufficient evidence. The fact rows use typed values, such as text or numbers, so a

00:01:26.504 --> 00:01:31.654
predicate can compare them consistently. The schema describes the shape; the attribute

00:01:31.654 --> 00:01:34.050
definition explains how to interpret it.

00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:43.746
The original shipment fields and the synthetic fact fields live in different field

00:01:43.746 --> 00:01:48.753
maps. A bridge describes how they relate: shipment identifier on one side, fact

00:01:48.753 --> 00:01:54.203
shipment identifier on the other. One shipment may have several facts, so the relation

00:01:54.203 --> 00:01:59.463
is one to many. The lens can expose this relation just like any other. An agent can

00:01:59.463 --> 00:02:04.850
discover it, traverse it in a predicate, and see which fields it is permitted to use.

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:14.102
Now build the question gradually. Require Express priority. Require at least two

00:02:14.102 --> 00:02:19.091
customs holds. Then require a related fact whose attribute is intervention and whose

00:02:19.091 --> 00:02:24.141
value is ownership handoff. The nested all group inside the fact relation means those

00:02:24.141 --> 00:02:28.893
last two tests must match the same fact row. One predicate now combines recorded

00:02:28.893 --> 00:02:33.764
information with an AI interpretation. The interpretation participates in the rule

00:02:33.764 --> 00:02:37.150
language instead of remaining only a paragraph in a chat.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:46.687
The source query first retrieves records within the lens. The permitted synthetic

00:02:46.687 --> 00:02:51.973
facts are connected to those records, and Check evaluates the combined predicate. Two

00:02:51.973 --> 00:02:57.259
records match. We can ask a more specific question using a window: order the holds by

00:02:57.259 --> 00:03:02.358
delay, skip the longest, and inspect the next one. That leaves one match. A window

00:03:02.358 --> 00:03:07.582
selects a portion of an ordered list. Each stage is explicit, inspectable, and still

00:03:07.582 --> 00:03:09.075
constrained by the lens.

00:03:13.575 --> 00:03:18.686
Now the platform removes the synthetic-fact relation from its lens. The combined

00:03:18.686 --> 00:03:24.116
predicate still tries to traverse that relation, so it is no longer valid. The source

00:03:24.116 --> 00:03:29.483
records still exist, and a source-only predicate still works. This is rule staleness

00:03:29.483 --> 00:03:34.977
caused by upstream narrowing. It does not depend on a clock. The agent must revise its

00:03:34.977 --> 00:03:40.216
predicate to fit the new surface, just as it would after losing access to a source

00:03:40.216 --> 00:03:40.600
field.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.835
Finally, attach an invented fact to another customer’s record. It cannot bring that

00:03:49.835 --> 00:03:54.571
record into the allowed result. The engine checks scope and provenance before using

00:03:54.571 --> 00:03:59.477
the fact. This is how the pieces fit together: an agent discovers a permitted surface,

00:03:59.477 --> 00:04:03.928
proposes interpretations and predicates, and receives results through enforced

00:04:03.928 --> 00:04:08.150
boundaries. We used shipping notes to make the reasoning visible. The same

00:04:08.150 --> 00:04:13.000
architecture can support your own entities, relationships, and AI-assisted workflows.
